window.MAYA_KB_GRAMMAR={"version":1,"note":"Deterministic construction grammar distilled from Patternmaking for Fashion Design, 5e (Joseph-Armstrong). These are HARD constraints injected into the grounded pattern prompt. Each type lists the canonical panel set, symmetry rule, and the proportion/measure facts the image model otherwise hallucinates. cite = book page the rule comes from.","symmetry_legend":{"bilateral":"left and right halves are mirror images; cut as mirrored pairs from one pattern piece","front_back_distinct":"front and back panels are different shapes and are NOT interchangeable","single":"one unique piece, cut once on the fold or flat"},"types":{"pants":{"match":["pant","pants","trouser","trousers","jean","jeans","slack","slacks","culotte","legging","chino","cargo","shorts"],"chapter":26,"summary":"A pant is BIFURCATED \u2014 divided into a right and a left leg. Each leg is built from a FRONT and a BACK panel. The back is cut wider than the front. Minimum 4 leg panels + a waistband.","panels":[{"label":"FRONT LEG","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"front = 1/4 of front hip; narrower than back; holds the crease line and crotch curve (shallow front rise)"},{"label":"BACK LEG","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"back = 1/2 of back hip; WIDER than the front; deeper/longer crotch curve and higher back rise; often 1-2 back darts"},{"label":"WAISTBAND","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"long rectangle = full waist circumference + 1-1.5\" underlap for closure; finished depth typically 1.25-2\" (cut x2 height + fold, or with interfacing)"}],"components":[{"label":"FLY FACING","qty":1,"when":"fly-front closure"},{"label":"FLY SHIELD","qty":1,"when":"fly-front closure"},{"label":"FRONT POCKET BAG","qty":2,"when":"slant/scoop front pockets"},{"label":"POCKET FACING","qty":2,"when":"front pockets"},{"label":"COIN POCKET","qty":1,"when":"5-pocket jean"},{"label":"BACK PATCH POCKET","qty":2,"when":"jean / casual"},{"label":"BACK YOKE","qty":1,"when":"jean with yoke (cut as pair if seamed at CB)"},{"label":"BELT LOOP","qty":5,"when":"belt loops present"}],"symmetry":"bilateral","facts":["Bifurcated = divided into right and left sides (cite p659).","Foundations differ only by crotch-extension length: culotte (longest) > trouser > slack > jean (shortest) (cite p660).","Back hip share > front hip share for every foundation; e.g. trouser back = 1/2 back hip, front = 1/4 front hip (cite p661).","Front waist = hip/4 + 1/4\"; back waist = hip/4 - 1/4\"; dart intake = hip - waist (cite p662).","Rise = crotch depth, measured waist-to-crotch level; back rise sits higher than front (cite p659,p663)."],"common_hallucinations":["Drawing only 3 leg panels instead of 4 (a leg needs BOTH a front and a back).","Making the front and back the same width \u2014 the back must be visibly wider.","A stubby/short waistband \u2014 it must span the FULL waist plus closure underlap.","Symmetric front/back \u2014 pants are mirror-symmetric LEFT/RIGHT, not top/bottom."]},"skirt":{"match":["skirt","a-line","pencil skirt","wrap skirt","circle skirt","pleated skirt","midi","maxi"],"chapter":13,"summary":"Basic skirt = a FRONT and a BACK panel shaped from waist to hem, plus a waistband. Four foundations: straight/rectangular, A-line/triangular, pegged, and circle.","panels":[{"label":"FRONT SKIRT","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"cut on the fold at center front; 1-2 waist darts per side for waist-to-hip shaping (or no darts on a flared/circle skirt)"},{"label":"BACK SKIRT","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"cut as a pair at center-back seam (for zipper/walking ease); 1-2 waist darts per side"},{"label":"WAISTBAND","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"full waist circumference + closure underlap; finished depth ~1-1.5\""}],"components":[{"label":"FACING","qty":2,"when":"faced waist instead of band"},{"label":"POCKET BAG","qty":2,"when":"side-seam pockets"},{"label":"GODET","qty":0,"when":"godet inserts (count per design)"}],"symmetry":"bilateral","facts":["Four foundations: straight, A-line/triangular, pegged, circle (cite p273ff).","Waist-hip difference is absorbed by darts (straight/A-line) or by flare (circle); a true circle skirt has NO darts.","Circle-skirt radius is derived from waist measurement, not drafted with darts."],"common_hallucinations":["Adding darts to a circle skirt.","Forgetting the center-back seam where the zipper goes."]},"shirt":{"match":["shirt","blouse","button-down","button up","top","buttondown","polo","tunic"],"chapter":21,"summary":"Button shirt = LEFT and RIGHT front (with placket extension), a back (often with a yoke), two set-in sleeves, a two-part collar (collar + stand), and cuffs.","panels":[{"label":"FRONT","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"left/right fronts; each carries half the button placket extension at center front"},{"label":"BACK","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"cut on the fold at center back; gathered or pleated into the yoke"},{"label":"YOKE","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"often cut double (self + facing); spans shoulder to upper back"},{"label":"SLEEVE","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"set-in sleeve with cap ease eased into the armhole; placket slit at wrist"},{"label":"COLLAR","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"cut x2 (upper + under collar)"},{"label":"COLLAR STAND","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"cut x2; carries the top button"},{"label":"CUFF","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"cut x2 each (self + facing)"}],"components":[{"label":"SLEEVE PLACKET","qty":2,"when":"tower/bound placket"},{"label":"FRONT PLACKET BAND","qty":2,"when":"separate placket"},{"label":"CHEST POCKET","qty":1,"when":"patch pocket"}],"symmetry":"bilateral","facts":["Two major sleeve classes: set-in (cut separately, eased into armhole) vs kimono/raglan (cite p349).","Sleeve cap carries ease distributed over the armhole.","Collar is two parts on a tailored shirt: the collar leaf and the stand (cite p515ff)."],"common_hallucinations":["A single front panel (a button shirt needs left AND right fronts).","A one-piece collar with no stand on a tailored shirt.","Forgetting the yoke."]},"jacket":{"match":["jacket","coat","blazer","outerwear","trench","parka","overcoat","suit jacket"],"chapter":22,"summary":"Tailored jacket = left/right fronts (carrying the lapel), a back (1 piece or split at CB), side panels (three-panel style), TWO-PIECE set-in sleeves (upper + under), a notch or shawl collar, plus facings and lining.","panels":[{"label":"FRONT","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"carries the lapel roll line and front dart/princess shaping; button extension at CF"},{"label":"SIDE FRONT","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"three-panel style: princess seam splits the front for shaping"},{"label":"BACK","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"center-back seam for shaping; back waist dart/suppression"},{"label":"UPPER SLEEVE","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"tailored two-piece sleeve; the larger outer half"},{"label":"UNDER SLEEVE","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"the smaller inner half; the seam hides at the underarm"},{"label":"TOP COLLAR","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"notch or shawl; cut x2 with undercollar"},{"label":"UNDERCOLLAR","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"often cut on the bias, sometimes 2 halves"},{"label":"FRONT FACING","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"finishes the lapel and front edge; carries to the lining"}],"components":[{"label":"WELT / FLAP POCKET","qty":2,"when":"front pockets"},{"label":"POCKET BAG","qty":2,"when":"front pockets"},{"label":"BREAST WELT","qty":1,"when":"chest welt pocket"},{"label":"LINING","qty":0,"when":"fully lined (mirrors the shell panels)"},{"label":"SLEEVE VENT","qty":2,"when":"functional/decorative cuff vent"}],"symmetry":"bilateral","facts":["Two jacket foundations; tailored jackets use the TWO-PIECE sleeve (upper + under), not a one-piece sleeve (cite p537ff).","Double-breasted = two parallel button columns ~2\" apart horizontally; single-breasted = one column (cite backend dissection rules).","Three-panel style adds a side-front panel via a princess seam for waist suppression."],"common_hallucinations":["A one-piece sleeve on a tailored jacket.","Forgetting the front facing that finishes the lapel.","A single back panel when the design clearly has a CB seam."]},"bodice":{"match":["bodice","dress","gown","fitted top","corset","leotard"],"chapter":3,"summary":"Basic foundation = front bodice, back bodice, front skirt, back skirt, and slim sleeves. Darts are directed toward the bust, waist, shoulder blade and elbow to bridge the body's hollows.","panels":[{"label":"FRONT BODICE","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"on the fold at CF; bust dart + waist dart"},{"label":"BACK BODICE","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"CB seam; shoulder/blade dart + waist dart"},{"label":"FRONT SKIRT","qty":1,"mirror":false,"note":"hangs from the hip; waist darts"},{"label":"BACK SKIRT","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"CB seam; waist darts"},{"label":"SLEEVE","qty":2,"mirror":true,"note":"set-in, slim full-length"}],"components":[{"label":"FACING","qty":2,"when":"neckline/armhole facings"}],"symmetry":"bilateral","facts":["The basic dress has five distinct parts: front + back bodice, front + back skirt, sleeves (cite p43).","Darts point toward the bust, abdomen, buttocks, shoulder blades and elbows to bridge hollow areas (cite p43)."],"common_hallucinations":["Drawing a dartless fitted bodice.","Forgetting the bust dart."]}},"adaptive_fallback":{"note":"When the vision classifier returns a garment that matches none of the named types, build the grammar from these principles instead of guessing.","principles":["Decide the symmetry first: nearly all garments are bilaterally symmetric (left = mirror of right). Cut shared shapes as mirrored pairs, not as one lopsided panel.","A garment that wraps a tube of the body (sleeve, leg, neck) needs a front AND a back surface \u2014 never a single panel for a 3D tube.","Any waistband, cuff, collar band or hem band is a long strip = the circumference it closes + a closure underlap; it is NOT a short stub.","Closures (button plackets, fly, center-back zip) split an otherwise-on-the-fold panel into a left/right pair.","Shaping over a body bulge (bust, seat, blade) is absorbed by darts, princess seams, or added flare \u2014 pick one and show it consistently.","Match the panel count to the visible seams in the reference image; if you can see a princess seam or a yoke, it must appear as separate panels."]}};
